# Protected paths — the refinery refuses to merge any branch that changes one.
#
# Read by internal/refinery/protectedpath_gate.go, from the TARGET ref (main),
# never from the branch under merge. A branch cannot exempt itself by editing
# this file, because editing this file is itself refused.
#
# THERE IS NO BYPASS. No flag, no label, no CODEOWNERS approval, no marker
# file. On this machine every agent holds a GH_TOKEN authenticating as the repo
# owner, so any token an agent could present is one an agent could forge; an
# authorisation mechanism here would be an opt-out with extra steps. A change
# to a protected path lands by hand-push from Daniel, which never enters the
# refinery. See the WHY NO BYPASS comment in protectedpath_gate.go.
#
# Syntax: one pattern per line, `#` comments, blank lines ignored.
#
#   dir/**        the whole subtree
#   dir/file.md   one exact repo-relative path
#   dir/*.sh      a single path segment glob
#
# An unrecognised pattern fails the gate rather than being skipped — a red line
# that silently matches nothing is worse than no red line at all.
#
# This file lives at the repo root and NOT under .pogo/, which is gitignored
# here and in most repos pogo manages. An untracked list is invisible to the
# gate (it reads git, not the working tree) and would silently protect nothing.
# TestShippedProtectedListIsParseable asserts it is tracked, so the mistake
# cannot be made quietly.

# Shipped agent prompt templates. Daniel's red line: mg-2a50 is the incident in
# which the refinery merged a +38-line edit to mayor.md (a3f0efa, mg-1935) with
# nothing objecting, while three other prompt tickets sat correctly frozen
# awaiting his authorisation.
internal/agent/prompts/**
internal/agent/templates/**

# This file. Listed for the reader's benefit; the gate protects it implicitly
# whether or not this line is present, so deleting the line does not unlock it.
.protected-paths
